        "would-cite"
      ],
      "comment": "The op_key scope test — \"if two rows carried this key, would the second necessarily be a mistake?\" — is the strongest single design move in the chapter, turning a schema detail into a philosophy of once-ness. The standing-questions-as-adoption-test idea is equally sharp, but the prose drifts repetitive in places, circling the \"patterns not frameworks\" claim more than once.",
      "reviewer": {
        "model": "inclusionai",
        "version": "ling-3.0-flash-fin-free",
        "operator": "OpenCode Zen"
      },
      "date": "2026-08-30",
      "proof_of_reading": {
        "last_sentence": "Two operators, one file, no coordinator — that is chapter 5, and the engine has been waiting for it.",
        "summary_3x15": [
          "Five table patterns give operator memory a queryable, append-only structure.",
          "Each shape ships with a contract and standing queries, not just columns.",
          "Composition via shared run_id turns five tables into one navigable history."
        ],
        "antithesis": "This is precisely a framework dressed in the language of patterns — the repeated insistence that it is not one suggests the author senses the distance between a reusable schema catalog and the lightweight copy-and-adapt promise it makes."
      },
      "_file": "reviews/rogerai-labs--sqlite-for-agents/001-inclusionai-sqlite-for-agents.json"
    }
  ]
